---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import results from "../data/results.json";
import historicalDatasets from "../data/historical-datasets.json";

const suites = (results.suites ?? {}) as Record<string, any>;

// Compute table rows in frontmatter — conditional bare <tr> expressions break
// Astro's HTML compiler (table elements get hoisted out of expressions).
const suiteRows = Object.entries(suites).map(([name, s]: [string, any]) => ({
  name: historicalDatasets[name]?.name ?? name,
  n: s.n_items,
  src: historicalDatasets[name]?.source ?? "Source unknown",
  uniqueInputs: historicalDatasets[name]?.unique_inputs ?? "Unknown",
  inputStatus: historicalDatasets[name]?.input_status ?? "Unknown",
  balance: historicalDatasets[name]?.balance ?? "Unknown",
  task: `${s.n_answers}-way choice`,
}));
---

<Base title="Methodology — Verdict">
  <h1>Methodology</h1>
  <p class="tagline">
    Verdict compares included provider adapters on labeled tasks with a common finite-choice contract:
    <em>given a question with a finite set of allowed answers and some context, choose exactly one answer</em>.
    Start with the task you need to solve, then inspect whether the measurements
    support a decision on that workload.
  </p>

  <h2>How to read the results</h2>
  <ol class="tagline">
    <li><strong>Same task, same inputs.</strong> Each model receives the same question,
      allowed answers, and input in that evaluation.</li>
    <li><strong>Check against expected answers.</strong> Benchmark accuracy compares choices
      with dataset labels. In the playground, you judge your own input; model agreement
      alone does not mean the answer is correct.</li>
    <li><strong>Check failures, time, and cost together.</strong> A useful model must return
      usable answers within your application's budget and response-time needs.
      Reported confidence is not a validated probability of correctness.</li>
  </ol>
  <p class="tagline">The recorded results describe these particular datasets and runs.
    To choose a model for your application, test representative inputs with reviewed
    expected answers. <a href="/playground">Try your own decision →</a></p>

  <h2>What our internal evaluation means</h2>
  <p class="tagline">Verdict ran these experiments and calculated the reported metrics.
    This is an internal evaluation, not an independent assessment or a claim of production reliability.</p>
  <ul class="tagline">
    <li><a href="/">Historical results</a>: six model/pipeline entries across four suites,
      totaling 4,746 saved observations. These records predate parser and accounting fixes.</li>
    <li><a href="/comparison">October 2 comparison</a>: Jev Direct, Clef, Clef Flash, and Nano
      on 24 unique, authored synthetic support tickets each (96 live calls). All matched the
      authored labels; this small set did not separate decision quality. It is a different
      dataset and provider roster, so it does not replace the historical tables.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>What we checked on October 4, 2026</h2>
  <ul class="tagline">
    <li>All 4,746 saved observations have consistent correctness flags and expected labels
      across providers. Headline historical table metrics match recalculation.</li>
    <li>Archived classification and moderation inputs, plus their 12 provider-result files,
      match the SHA-256 checksums saved during the October 1 archive. This confirms the files
      are unchanged since that archive.</li>
    <li>For classification and moderation, all 2,358 observations align with the archived
      item IDs, expected labels, and allowed choices. Each provider covers the same items,
      with no duplicate item IDs or blank inputs.</li>
    <li>Bundled agent-action and routing inputs also align with recorded expected labels
      and allowed choices. Their distinct-input counts and label balance are disclosed below.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="callout">This audit establishes file integrity, record alignment, and arithmetic consistency.
    It does not independently establish human label quality, original provider-call authenticity,
    source permissions, model provenance, calibrated confidence, or invoice accuracy.
    Missing raw outputs cannot be rechecked with the current parser.</p>

  <h2>The contract every provider must satisfy</h2>
  <pre><code>{`DecisionRequest:
  question:  str                  # the judgment to make
  answers:   list[str]            # finite, predefined answer set (verbatim choice required)
  context:   str | None           # text to judge

DecisionResponse:
  answer:      str | None         # must be one of the allowed answers
  confidence:  float | None       # 0..1, optional
  latency_ms, cost_usd            # measured per call`}</code></pre>
  <p class="tagline">
    Chat adapters share a decision prompt. OpenAI chat baselines request a strict
    <code>answer</code>-enum JSON schema; Solar requests JSON-object output, Ollama uses
    JSON mode, and Jev Router receives plain text. The provisional native adapter uses
    a separate question/answers/context payload and has no verified benchmark records.
    The shared parser accepts a single JSON decision (optionally wrapped in prose), a bare
    or quoted label, or a short explicit answer declaration. It rejects ambiguous prose rather than
    guessing from mentioned labels. Duplicate JSON keys are rejected, including nested and escaped keys.
    Reasoning-only responses are errors. JSON decisions may also use
    a zero-based answer index. Returned answers must belong to the supplied list.
  </p>

  <h2>Suites</h2>
  <p class="tagline">
    This table describes the historical benchmark suites. Only routing and synthetic agent examples
    are bundled. AG News and TweetEval hate raw text are excluded from public distributions and Git
    history; those suites accept permitted private local inputs.
  </p>
  <p class="tagline">
    Routing samples are adapted from <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Tobi-Bueck/customer-support-tickets">Tobi Bueck's Customer Support Tickets</a>
    under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0/">CC-BY-NC-4.0</a>, separately from the MIT code.
    Changes include sampling English rows, selecting queues, combining and truncating text, and adding
    decision fields. Dataset use is noncommercial, with attribution required; it is provided without
    warranties and without endorsement. The source describes synthetic ticket generation.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Historical dataset summary" tabindex="0">
  <table class="board">
    <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>Items</th><th>Unique inputs</th><th>Input status</th><th>Choices</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {suiteRows.map((r: any) => (
        <tr>
          <td>{r.name}</td>
          <td class="num">{r.n}</td>
          <td class="num">{r.uniqueInputs}</td>
          <td>{r.inputStatus}</td>
          <td class="num">{r.task}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <details class="dataset-details">
    <summary>Configured sources and label balance</summary>
    {suiteRows.map(row => <div><h3>{row.name}</h3><p>{row.src}</p><p>{row.balance}</p></div>)}
  </details>
  <p class="tagline">
    Source names reflect the original builder configuration, which supports generated fallbacks;
    they are not verified row-level provenance. Source revisions and row-level manifests were not
    retained. The agent-action suite has 200 rows but only 105 distinct inputs from 12 templates,
    and does not evaluate tool execution, prerequisites, or completed agent workflows.
  </p>

  <h2>Metrics</h2>
  <ul class="tagline">
    <li><strong>Accuracy</strong> — exact verbatim match against the labeled answer, over successful responses. Completion rate is successful responses / all items; all-item accuracy counts failures as incorrect. The leaderboard retains successful-response accuracy for ranking and shows completion separately.</li>
    <li><strong>Median / 95th-percentile response time (p50/p95)</strong> — p50 is the middle recorded time; p95 is the time at or below which 95% of observations fall. Future benchmark records measure total item wall time, including retries, backoff, and billing lookups; executor queue time is excluded. The saved snapshot predates this correction and used concurrency 4.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost per 1,000 items</strong> — total recorded cost / all items × 1,000, including failed items. This metric and total spend are unknown unless every item is priced. Cost coverage reports priced items / all items; the known subtotal remains a partial sum when coverage is incomplete. Future records include all retry costs. OpenRouter exact mode uses reported account charges, including zero; missing charges stay unknown. OpenAI uses published standard token-rate estimates with cache discounts. The displayed historical costs have not been repaired.</li>
    <li><strong>ECE</strong> — expected calibration error over 10 equal-width confidence bins, using successful items with reported confidence. Lower values mean a smaller observed gap between confidence and correctness on these samples; they do not validate thresholds on another workload.</li>
    <li><strong>Escalation curves</strong> — offline simulations joining recorded answers by item ID (GPT-6 Luna by default). A successful primary with confidence below the threshold attempts escalation; primary failures and missing confidence do not. Failed escalation retains the primary answer, while its cost and time are included. Costs are known per simulated item only if every contributing record is priced. Missing required escalation records make simulated accuracy, cost, and latency unknown; failed and missing counts are displayed. Latency sums recorded primary and attempted escalation durations, including failures; this does not measure a live cascade or validate thresholds on held-out data.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Disclosed caveats</h2>
  <ul class="tagline">
    <li><strong>Parser change.</strong> The initial committed benchmark snapshot used a more permissive parser. Raw model output was not saved, so those results cannot be revalidated offline with the stricter parser; a fresh benchmark is required.</li>
    <li><strong>Provider identity.</strong> Jev Router rows measure its routing pipeline, not native Jev decisions. The <span class="badge proxy">proxy</span> wraps the same Nano backend as the Nano baseline. Neither native Jev nor native OpenAI Decisions has measured rows here.</li>
    <li><strong>Routed-model provenance.</strong> New records retain requested and response-reported model IDs; missing IDs stay unknown. These are provider reports, not independent verification. Historical benchmark records omit model identity. Exact-cost mode now uses reported account charges; historical costs were recorded under the older resolver.</li>
    <li><strong>Confidence comparability.</strong> Chat confidence is model-reported. Nonfinite, boolean, or unparseable confidence becomes missing; finite numeric values are clamped to [0, 1]. Missing confidence is excluded from ECE and bypasses SDK escalation. Imported invalid confidence is also excluded from ECE and offline threshold simulations. These checks do not establish calibrated confidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Snapshot pricing.</strong> Standard token rates in <code>cost.py</code> were verified on 2026-10-01. Estimates exclude special service tiers, regional uplifts, and account adjustments. Saved records omit token usage and billing metadata, so old costs cannot be repriced offline. Summaries preserve unknown costs and disclose coverage.</li>
    <li><strong>Single-run variance.</strong> These are single-run observations without repeated-run uncertainty estimates. Remote adapters do not set temperature. A scheduled workflow exists, but verified nightly publication has not been established.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure coverage.</strong> Successful-response accuracy and ECE exclude failed responses. Completion rate and all-item accuracy disclose failures; latency and cost include them. Billing coverage means a cost value was recorded, not that its billing basis was independently verified. The saved snapshot contains no recorded errors, so failure handling is verified with offline fixtures rather than these historical rows.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Reproduce it</h2>
  <p class="tagline">Saved summaries can be rebuilt from historical records without inference.
    Rerunning classification and moderation requires permitted private inputs under
    their source terms. New measurements make paid provider calls.</p>
  <pre><code>{`cd python
uv sync --extra dev
# Rebuild the saved historical summary; no inference calls:
uv run verdict aggregate --results ../results --out ../site/src/data/results.json
cd ../site && npm run build

# New paid measurements, after checking source terms and spend:
cd ../python
uv run verdict bench --all --out ../new-results
# Defaults: routing + agent_next_action. Optional local suites require
# VERDICT_DATASET_DIR and an explicit --suite classification or moderation.`}</code></pre>
</Base>

<style>
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; }
  .dataset-details { margin: 18px 0; color: var(--muted); font-size: 14px; }
  .dataset-details summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--accent); }
</style>
